#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}___________ \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-3)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1:= \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1, \\qquad R_1:= R_1+(-3)R_2\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2:= R_2+(-1)R_1$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\ne I\\cdot \\\\\nB &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac77f48a-a30c-5076-afc5-9837ae5358bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113932} {:answer/id #uuid "0576d41c-6ee8-5d21-84da-b337818bd05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113933} {:answer/id #uuid "163d3c71-4381-5325-8ae6-f0169ec83a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113934} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbace26-eb7a-54f6-bb5f-7391d1eef516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113935} {:answer/id #uuid "45b1a83f-67b3-5525-9990-f05acc93c42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45b1a83f-67b3-5525-9990-f05acc93c42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113936} :answer-field/id #uuid "c56039e3-a2d1-5d0e-9666-15264ba157b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113931}] :question/id #uuid "2ae71b84-85fc-5e7b-937d-d496882effbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-42988" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
